+## 1d. The game's own writer refuses unguarded writes — SOURCE, and narrower than it reads
+
+`src/core/save_game.gd:305-312`:
+
+    static func _fixture_may_not_write(path: String) -> bool:
+        if not path.begins_with("user://"):        return false
+        if not OS.get_environment("SF_ISOLATED_HOME").is_empty():  return false
+        if OS.get_environment("SF_REAL_HOME") == "1":              return false
+        return OS.get_cmdline_args().has("--script")
+
+Good design: a positive marker, so absence of proof of isolation is the refusal condition. Three limits,
+all from the source:
+
+- it guards `SaveGame.write` only. A `FileAccess.open(...)` or `DirAccess.remove_absolute(...)` anywhere
+  else is untouched by it;
+- `--script` is the fixture tell. **A judged actor driven by a scene, an autoload, or `--main-pack`
+  carries no `--script`, so this refusal would not fire for it at all;**
+- it is disarmed by `SF_ISOLATED_HOME` being merely *set*. If `export HOME=` at `with_machine.sh:60` were
+  deleted while `export SF_ISOLATED_HOME=` at line 69 survived, `user://` would be the player's directory
+  and this guard would report the run as sandboxed. Nothing checks the pair.
+
+## 1e. "cannot NAME the production slot" is a guard over one spelling — VERIFIED
+
+`tools/check_save_isolation.gd` is a registered layer (`run_harness.sh:267`, first in the list) and it is a
+good one: recursive over `tools/` and `tests/`, so `tools/eval/` is inside its population — 127 `.gd` under
+`tools/` and 5 under `tests/`, of which the layer scans 130 (it exempts itself and `save_sentinel.gd`)
+against its floor of 40. Non-vacuity is proved in both directions and the splice-flattener is proved to
+fire on a sample built for it.
+
+Its subject is the literal `"user://sinkforge.save"` (line 38) and a `"user://…​.save"` regex (line 114). I
+ran both matchers over the ways a script could reach the player's file:
+
+    $ cat probe.txt
+    var a := "user://sinkforge.save"
+    var b := "user://" + "sinkforge.save"
+    var c := "/Users/thondascully/Library/Application Support/Godot/app_userdata/Sinkforge/sinkforge.save"
+    var d := OS.get_environment("HOME") + "/Library/.../Sinkforge/sinkforge.save"
+    var e := "/Users/thondascully/Library/.../Sinkforge/sinkforge.save.bak"
+
+    $ grep -n -F 'user://sinkforge.save' probe.txt                       # the raw contains(SLOT)
+    1:var a := "user://sinkforge.save"
+
+    $ sed -E 's/"[[:space:]]*\+[[:space:]]*"//g' probe.txt | grep -n -F 'user://sinkforge.save'
+    1:var a := "user://sinkforge.save"
+    2:var b := "user://sinkforge.save"
+
+    $ sed -E 's/"[[:space:]]*\+[[:space:]]*"//g' probe.txt \
+        | grep -n -oE '"(user://[A-Za-z0-9_%./-]+\.save)"'              # the _slots() regex
+    1:"user://sinkforge.save"
+    2:"user://sinkforge.save"
+
+**Lines c, d and e match nothing, under either matcher.** The layer's own docstring says as much at lines
+22-27 ("it still cannot see a path built from a variable or a format string at runtime") and names
+`save_sentinel.gd` as the half that covers it. That is honest, and 1f is where that cover runs out.
+
+Line `e` is the sharper one: it is not an evasion, it is the **sibling file the game itself reads**.
+`save_game.gd:36` defines `BAK_SUFFIX = ".bak"` and line 23 says a slot that is "missing, truncated or
+malformed falls back to `.bak`". A real player save is at least two files. Nothing here, and nothing in 1f,
+watches the second one.
