+## 1f. The empirical witness does not travel with the run the gate is about — SOURCE
+
+`tools/save_sentinel.gd` is the instrument that makes "the harness never touched your save" **proved**
+rather than true-by-construction. It hashes the real slot before and after. Its subject is
+`SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT` (line 106), and:
+
+    $ grep -rn 'SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT' --exclude-dir=.git .
+    tools/save_sentinel.gd:106  ...    tools/save_sentinel.gd:219  ...
+    tools/run_harness.sh:180    export SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT="$HOME/Library/.../sinkforge.save"
+    tools/run_harness.sh:182    export SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/godot/..."
+
+**`with_machine.sh` never sets it.** Under `with_machine.sh` the witness reads `NO_WITNESS` (`"-"`, line
+108) and the sentinel prints *"verified — the run's own slot is untouched (no production slot named)"*.
+The hard rule for this lane, and the only sane rule for a journey run, is that everything boots through
+`with_machine.sh`. **So for the agent-journey run specifically, the production witness is unarmed, and the
+sentinel is not invoked at all** — nothing outside `run_harness.sh` calls it (`arm` at line 1180, `verify`
+at 1364, `disarm` at 988).
+
+Three further limits, all from the source:
+
+- **It witnesses one file.** `SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT` names `sinkforge.save`. Not `.bak` (1e), not
+  `settings.cfg` (`scenes/settings.gd:33`, and it is 120 bytes of the player's real audio and feel
+  preferences dated 19 Aug), not `logs/`, not the ten other `user://*.save` fixture slots.
+- **Absent-to-absent passes, and that is the state of this machine.** There is no `sinkforge.save` in the
+  player's directory (see the `find` in 1c). `_production_digest()` returns `"absent"`; `verify` compares
+  `"absent" == "absent"` and passes, printing *"absent throughout"*. A run that created the file and then
+  deleted it scores identically. The sentinel's own header, lines 22-28, identifies exactly this hazard for
+  the `user://` slot and pays for it with a planted marker. **The production witness has no plant** — it
+  must not write there — so the hazard is unmitigated on the production side, by necessity rather than by
+  oversight. On this box today, that is not a corner case; it is the only case.
+- **A hash is invariant under reading.** Of the gate's three verbs, "cannot **read**" has no instrument at
+  all. Nothing in this repository detects or prevents a read of the player's save. It is prevented in
+  practice only by the redirect, and only for code that spells the path with `user://`.
+
+## 1g. The lock — SOURCE, well tested, and per-invocation rather than per-session
+
+`with_machine.sh` acquires by `mkdir "$LOCK"` (line 96), writes a four-line owner file (line 133), and
+releases from an EXIT/INT/TERM trap (line 179) that releases **only a lock it still owns**
+(`lock_lib.sh:64-68`). The stale sweep runs under its own mutex with a verify-and-put-back recovery
+(`lock_lib.sh:96-143`), and the reasoning for why an atomic rename is not sufficient is written out and is
+correct.
+
+`tools/check_lock.sh` holds it to 26 properties, is registered at `run_harness.sh:355` through
+`add_excl_hl` (alone, no surface), and needs no Godot — it drives the wrapper against a stub engine and its
+own `SF_LOCK`. **I did not run it**, because it invokes `with_machine.sh` and the standing instruction is no
+`with_machine.sh` runs. It is the strongest single piece of evidence available for this clause and it is
+one command away.
+
+**UNVERIFIED — the exact run that settles it:** `bash tools/check_lock.sh`, which must end
+`check_lock: PASS — the machine lock holds, and giving up is not a pass` and exit 0.
+
+What check_lock does **not** cover, read off its own case list: it says nothing about `HOME`, nothing about
+`user://`, and nothing about the save. It is a lock test. The two halves of gate 1 have one test between
+them and it is the half that does not concern the player's data.
+
+**"for its whole session" is the part that does not hold as written.** Three separate reasons, all from
+source:
+
+- **The lock is per-invocation.** It is taken at line 96 and released at line 179 of one
+  `with_machine.sh` call. A journey session assembled from more than one invocation — boot, then capture,
+  then a second scenario — is unlocked in the gaps, and another lane can take the box there.
+- **`SF_RUN_CAP` defaults to 1800s** (line 153) and a capped run exits 6 having been SIGTERMed then
+  SIGKILLed (`cap_lib.sh:51-60`). The protocol's run is *20 minutes of normal game time* with an actor in
+  the loop; wall-clock for an LLM-driven actor is not game-clock. A session that overruns 30 minutes is
+  killed mid-run, and the receipt for that is `DRIVER_INVALID`, not a result.
+- **`SF_LOCK_WAIT` defaults to 900s** (line 38). A 20-minute session holding the box makes every concurrent
+  harness run give up at 15 minutes with exit 5. Correct behaviour, and an operational fact the protocol
+  should state: a journey run takes the machine off the air for its duration.
+
+Documented bypasses, all honoured silently and none recorded in any receipt: `SF_NO_LOCK=1`
+(`run_harness.sh:1142`, `seed_corpus.sh:89` — not honoured by `with_machine.sh`), `SF_LOCK=<path>` (a
+private lock that serialises with nobody), `SF_REAL_HOME=1` (no isolation whatsoever, and it also switches
+off `save_game.gd`'s refusal and the sentinel's).
+
+## Gate 1 verdict: **NARROWER-THAN-CLAIMED**
+
+What is genuinely established: `user://` follows `HOME` on this box (verified from artifacts on disk); the
+redirect fails closed in source and was already repaired once by this gate; a good source scan keeps the
+`user://` spelling of the player's slot out of 132 fixture files; the machine lock is sound and has 26
+properties behind it.
+
+Read against the gate's actual sentence, over the population the gate names:
+
+| clause | holds for | does not hold for |
+|---|---|---|
+| cannot **read** a real player save | nothing | no instrument exists; a hash cannot see a read |
+| cannot **overwrite** | `user://`-spelled paths, in wrapped runs | absolute or composed paths; `.bak`; `settings.cfg`; unwrapped runs |
+| cannot **delete** | same | same, plus: absent-to-absent passes, which is this machine's state |
+| **owns the lock for its whole session** | one `with_machine.sh` invocation, up to 1800s | multi-invocation sessions; overruns; `SF_LOCK`/`SF_NO_LOCK` |
+
+The three things that would most change this verdict, in order:
+
+1. A layer that asserts the wrappers export `HOME` **and** `SF_ISOLATED_HOME` together, and that the second
+   is never exported without the first. That pair is the quiet-green: isolation off, guard on.
+2. `with_machine.sh` exporting `SF_PRODUCTION_SLOT` the way `run_harness.sh` does, so the witness travels
+   with the runs the journey evaluation will actually make.
+3. A witness over the save **family** (`sinkforge.save`, `.bak`, `settings.cfg`) rather than one path.
+
